<Project Sdk="Godot.NET.Sdk/4.7.1">

  <!--
    CMP-APP-001, CMP-PRE-001, CMP-PRE-002, CMP-UI-001, CMP-AUD-001, CMP-PLT-001.
    The Godot composition root, input adapters, presentation, UI, audio, and
    platform adapters.

    Accepted project boundary (docs/technical/115 § Accepted project boundary):
      allowed project dependencies: all pure projects plus Godot APIs
      Godot types allowed:          Yes

    This is the ONLY project in the repository allowed to reference Godot.
    The engine version is pinned by the Sdk attribute above (TR-FND-001,
    TR-BLD-001) and locked in packages.lock.json.

    MechaMiner.Tools is deliberately not referenced: it is a build/tool-process
    library, and the game must not depend on tool workflows.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Required so Godot can load the game assembly into its own AssemblyLoadContext. -->
    <EnableDynamicLoading>true</EnableDynamicLoading>
    <RootNamespace>MechaMiner.Game</RootNamespace>
    <!--
      Redundant with Directory.Build.props on purpose. The Godot editor's C#
      project check reads the target framework from this file directly; when the
      property is only inherited, a headless Godot import rewrites the csproj to
      add it and leaves a MechaMiner.Game.csproj.old backup behind. Declaring it
      here keeps a headless import from mutating a committed project file. It must
      stay equal to the repository pin.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <!--
      Reflection-based System.Text.Json is off, matching every test host under
      tests/. This project emits a runtimeconfig.json despite being loaded as a
      library, because EnableDynamicLoading above is true; the property lands in
      its configProperties (verified against the emitted file).

      Godot loads this assembly into its own AssemblyLoadContext inside the
      Godot process, so at play time the switch that actually binds is the one in
      the hosting process's runtimeconfig. Declaring it here is what makes the
      constraint uniform across everything the repository builds and is what an
      exported, self-hosted build reads; it is not a claim about the editor's
      process.

      Do not move this property onto a plain library such as those under src/:
      those emit no runtimeconfig.json, so the switch goes silently dead in every
      host (measured). A shared props file that each host imports - such as the
      root Directory.Build.props - does bind and is allowed. Full rule, and the
      runtime assertion the test hosts owe, in
      tests/MechaMiner.Content.Tests/MechaMiner.Content.Tests.csproj.
    -->
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>false</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
    <!--
      Every restore of THIS project is locked, not only the ones that pass the
      locked-mode switch on the command line. This project alone needs it,
      because this project alone has a configuration-dependent package graph.

      Godot.NET.Sdk gates its GodotSharpEditor PackageReference on
      Condition=" '$(Configuration)' == 'Debug' " (Sdk.targets:37). So a restore
      at Release, ExportDebug or ExportRelease resolves a SMALLER graph than the
      committed lock file records, and - because Directory.Build.props sets
      RestorePackagesWithLockFile=true - NuGet rewrites packages.lock.json to
      match, dropping the GodotSharpEditor entry. Measured: that rewrite is
      silent. `dotnet build game/MechaMiner.Game.csproj -c Release` exited 0,
      printed "Build succeeded / 0 Warning(s) / 0 Error(s)", and emitted not one
      line mentioning the lock file. The damage surfaced much later and
      elsewhere, as VER-FND-001-001 (a locked-mode `dotnet restore
      MechaMiner.sln`) failing NU1004 on a tree in which nobody edited a source
      file.

      RestoreLockedMode=true converts that silent rewrite into an immediate
      NU1004 naming GodotSharpEditor, raised by the very command that caused it,
      and leaves the lock file byte-identical. The failure moves from a distant
      misattributed gate failure to a local one whose message already names the
      cause.

      Consequence, intended: `dotnet build game/MechaMiner.Game.csproj` at any
      configuration other than Debug now FAILS, and so does
      `dotnet build MechaMiner.sln -c Release` (which maps this project to
      ExportRelease). Both used to exit 0 while corrupting the lock file. The
      sanctioned solution build passes the no-restore switch
      (VER-FND-001-002) and is unaffected; build/verify-godot.sh cannot, because
      it deletes game/.godot for a cold cache and that is where this project's
      obj/ lives, so it must restore - which it does at the default
      configuration, Debug, and therefore stays green. When the graph
      legitimately changes, the escape hatch is a restore passing
      force-evaluate: explicit, reviewable, and visible in the diff.

      Do NOT "fix" this by setting RestorePackagesWithLockFile=false here. That
      would delete the guarantee rather than enforce it, leaving the one project
      with a genuinely configuration-dependent graph as the only project with no
      locked graph at all.

      Known limit, measured, so nobody reads more into this than it does:
      locked mode validates the package reference SET (names and requested
      ranges) and does NOT validate the "resolved" version recorded in the lock
      file, nor does it fail when the lock file is absent.
    -->
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../src/MechaMiner.Content/MechaMiner.Content.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../src/MechaMiner.Simulation/MechaMiner.Simulation.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../src/MechaMiner.Persistence/MechaMiner.Persistence.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
